**Payroll process: N upserts in a loop** — `src/app/api/payroll/[id]/process/route.ts:41-57`

The worst offender. Each employee triggers a separate `upsert`, meaning N+1 database roundtrips for a tenant with N employees. The pool can be saturated with large teams.

```ts
// Current: N individual transactions
const payrollItems = await Promise.all(
  employees.map(async (emp) =>
    prisma.payrollItem.upsert({ ... }) // 1 query per employee
  )
);

// Better: split into batch create + batch update
const existingIds = new Set(
  (await prisma.payrollItem.findMany({
    where: { payrollRunId: id },
    select: { employeeId: true },
  })).map((r) => r.employeeId)
);

const toCreate = employees.filter((e) => !existingIds.has(e.id));
const toUpdate = employees.filter((e) => existingIds.has(e.id));

await prisma.$transaction([
  prisma.payrollItem.createMany({ data: toCreate.map(computeItem) }),
  ...toUpdate.map((e) =>
    prisma.payrollItem.update({ where: { payrollRunId_employeeId: { payrollRunId: id, employeeId: e.id } }, data: computeItem(e) })
  ),
]);
```

**Employee code generation: count + create race condition** — `src/app/api/employees/route.ts:44-46`

Two concurrent POST requests can read the same count and produce duplicate codes. Use a DB sequence or move the code to a `GENERATED ALWAYS` column.

```ts
// Current — racy
const count = await prisma.employee.count({ where: { tenantId } });
const employeeCode = `EMP${String(count + 1).padStart(4, "0")}`;

// Better — atomic, no extra query needed
// Add a DB sequence in your migration:
// CREATE SEQUENCE emp_code_seq;
// Then in create:
const employeeCode = await prisma.$queryRaw<[{ nextval: bigint }]>`
  SELECT nextval('emp_code_seq')`.then((r) => `EMP${String(r[0].nextval).padStart(4, "0")}`);
```

**Leaves POST: sequential tenant-ownership checks** — `src/app/api/leaves/route.ts:39-44`

Two queries run one after the other when they could be parallel:

```ts
// Current — sequential
const employee = await prisma.employee.findFirst(...);
const leaveType = await prisma.leaveType.findFirst(...);

// Better — parallel
const [employee, leaveType] = await Promise.all([
  prisma.employee.findFirst({ where: { id: employeeId, tenantId } }),
  prisma.leaveType.findFirst({ where: { id: leaveTypeId, tenantId } }),
]);
```

---

**`fetchRuns` / `fetchLeaves` / `fetchRecords` recreated every render** — `payroll/page.tsx:42`, `leaves/page.tsx:37`, `attendance/page.tsx:37`

These functions are declared inside the component body without `useCallback`, so a new function reference is created on every render. Not critical now, but any child that receives them as a prop would re-render needlessly.

```tsx
// Current
const fetchRuns = () => { ... };

// Better
const fetchRuns = useCallback(() => { ... }, []);
```

**`handleTerminate` closure leak** — `employees/page.tsx:48`

Same issue — also captures `setEmployees` which is stable, but the closure itself is unstabilized.

**`total` computed inline in render** — `payroll/page.tsx:97`

```tsx
// Current — re-computed every render for every run
const total = run.payrollItems.reduce((s, i) => s + Number(i.netSalary), 0);

// Better — precompute when data arrives
const runsWithTotals = useMemo(
  () => runs.map((r) => ({ ...r, total: r.payrollItems.reduce((s, i) => s + Number(i.netSalary), 0) })),
  [runs]
);
```

---

**Dashboard server queries run on every navigation** — `src/app/(dashboard)/page.tsx:17-34`

All four queries hit the DB on every page visit. These stats are stale-safe for 30–60 seconds.

```ts
import { unstable_cache } from "next/cache";

const getDashboardStats = unstable_cache(
  async (tenantId: string) => {
    const [totalEmployees, presentToday, pendingLeaves, lastPayroll] = await Promise.all([...]);
    return { totalEmployees, presentToday, pendingLeaves, lastPayroll };
  },
  ["dashboard-stats"],
  { revalidate: 60, tags: ["dashboard"] }
);
```

Then invalidate the `"dashboard"` tag on attendance/leave mutations.

**Departments list is never cached** — `src/app/api/departments/route.ts`

Departments change rarely. The employee creation form fetches them every time. Add `Cache-Control: max-age=300` or use `unstable_cache` with a long TTL.

**`getServerSession` repeated in every route** — every API file

Each route calls `getServerSession(authOptions)` independently. NextAuth does cache the session per request in Next.js, but wrapping in a helper keeps the pattern consistent and makes future caching easier:

```ts
// src/lib/session.ts
import { cache } from "react";
export const getSession = cache(() => getServerSession(authOptions));
```

---

**No `AbortController` on fetch in `useEffect`** — `employees/page.tsx:37-46`, `leaves/page.tsx:45-47`, `attendance/page.tsx:45-47`, `payroll/page.tsx:50-53`

If the user navigates away before the fetch completes, the `.then()` callbacks attempt to call `setState` on an unmounted component. In React 18 this is a warning, not a hard error, but it wastes work and can mask bugs.

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch("/api/employees", { signal: controller.signal })
    .then((r) => r.json())
    .then((data) => { if (Array.isArray(data)) setEmployees(data); })
    .catch((e) => { if (e.name !== "AbortError") setError("Network error"); })
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

Same pattern needed in `fetchRecords` (attendance) and `fetchRuns`/`fetchLeaves` — though the refetch-after-mutation paths need the controller threaded through or scoped locally.

---

**Dashboard `lastPayrollTotal` could be a DB aggregate** — `src/app/(dashboard)/page.tsx:36-38`

You're fetching all payslip `netSalary` values into memory to sum them in JS. Use Prisma's aggregate instead:

```ts
// Current — fetches all items into memory
include: { payrollItems: { select: { netSalary: true } } }
// then JS reduce...

// Better — sum in the DB
prisma.payrollRun.findFirst({
  where: { tenantId, status: "COMPLETED" },
  orderBy: { runAt: "desc" },
  include: {
    _count: false,
    payrollItems: { select: { _count: true } }, // or
  },
});
// OR use a separate aggregate query:
prisma.payrollItem.aggregate({
  where: { payrollRun: { tenantId, status: "COMPLETED", runAt: { not: null } } },
  _sum: { netSalary: true },
  // + subquery for latest run period
});
```

**`statusVariant` functions duplicated across 4 components**

`employees/page.tsx:25`, `payroll/page.tsx:28`, `leaves/page.tsx:22`, `attendance/page.tsx:23` all define similar functions locally. No runtime cost, but extract to `src/lib/utils.ts` to eliminate the duplication maintenance risk.

